<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banteay Kdei</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prasat Banteay Kdei, Citadel of Chambers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Banteay Kdei, the Citadel of Chambers, is a Buddhist temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, southeast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> and east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a>, built between the mid-12th and early 13th centuries during the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>. A monastic complex in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> style, similar in plan to Ta Prohm and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/preah-khan">Preah Khan</a> but smaller and less complex, it stands on the site of a 10th-century temple of Rajendravarman, and inscriptions record its building by Jayavarman VII and the royal architect Kavindrarimathana. Monks occupied it at intervals over the centuries until the 1960s. Faulty construction and poor sandstone have left it dilapidated, and it is now under renovation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banteay-kdei.jpg" alt="Banteay Kdei" width="330" height="498" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diego Delso · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Banteay_Kdei,_Angkor,_Camboya,_2013-08-16,_DD_10.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Banteay Kdei stands in the Angkor Archaeological Park, 3 kilometres east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor-thom">Angkor Thom</a> and reached from the east gate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ta-prohm">Ta Prohm</a> by a path of 600 metres. The sanctuary is packed into a space of 65 by 50 metres within three enclosures and a laterite outer wall of 700 by 300 metres, on a single level.</p><p>Its structures consist of two concentric galleries from which towers rise, preceded to the east by a cloister. The east gate is cruciform and carries images of Lokesvara, and the carvings include apsaras in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayon">Bayon</a> style and, in the inner enclosure, traces of an earlier style. To the east lies Srah Srang, a pool dug under Rajendravarman in the 10th century and given laterite steps by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayavarman-vii">Jayavarman VII</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The name goes back to an earlier name, Kuti, found in the Sdok Kak Thom stele, which records an estate and village of that name granted to the family of the royal chaplain Sivakaivalya. Jayavarman VII came to power at the age of 55 after defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cham">Chams</a>, who had invaded and devastated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a>, and built many temples and rest houses for pilgrims.</p><p>Banteay Kdei is reported to be his first temple, of 1181. Small inscriptions name him and the royal architect Kavindrarimathana as its builders. In the 13th century most of his temples were vandalised, though some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana</a> frontons and lintels survive. Later the temple lay neglected under vegetation until it was cleared in 1920 to 1922 under Henri Marchal, using anastylosis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Monks occupied the temple at intervals until the 1960s. Its sandstone was poor and its construction faulty, and vaults have collapsed in places, so parts are cordoned off and tied together with cables during renovation. A Sophia University mission found a cache of fragments of 274 Buddhist sandstone statues in 2001, and from 2004 the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, Sophia University and the nonprofit CyArk made laser scans of the temple.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banteay Kdei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the reign of Jayavarman VII alone Ta Prohm, Preah Khan, <b>Banteay Kdei</b> and Neak Pean were built, beside the Bayon at the centre of Angkor Thom.</blockquote><a href="/societies/khmer-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Khmer Empire</a></li></ul></section>
